{
  "esm": "1.0.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "F2TopLevelRefDoesNotMergeIndexSets",
    "description": "FINDING F2 (expected to FAIL at the pinned toolchain). A top-level `models` entry that is a §4.7 `{ref}` does NOT merge the referenced document's top-level `index_sets` into the mounting document's registry.\n\nExpected diagnostic, from `esm validate` and again from the build:\n  Aggregate range references undeclared index set 'leaf_left'\n\nesm-spec §4.7 'Index-set merge' says it must: 'A referenced subsystem file's top-level index_sets merge into the importing document's document-scoped registry at resolution time ... This is what makes the mounted-mesh pattern sound: the importing model's variables may be shaped over the mesh file's axes without redeclaring them.' A NESTED `subsystems: {X: {ref}}` edge does merge them (the F1 repro validates cleanly, and fails later for a different reason); a top-level `models` `{ref}` edge does not.\n\nWhy it matters here: the two defects are complementary, and together they mean an assembly cannot both mount a relational leaf and inherit its axes. runs/micro_exhaust_run.esm takes the top-level form and restates the axes, and run-tests.sh compares an assembly's declarations against the referenced files' to catch the disagreement this merge exists to prevent.\n\nWhen this starts passing, delete the restated `index_sets` from runs/micro_exhaust_run.esm and the assembly-consistency stage from run-tests.sh.",
    "authors": ["EarthSciML Authors and Contributors"]
  },
  "models": {
    "Leaf": { "ref": "./join_leaf.esm" }
  }
}
